# The first write. Every other scenario here is judged by what it draws; this
# one is judged by one number in the [exi] report -- "N written", which has
# been 0 in every log this repository has ever kept.
name: cardwrite
summary: Blank card in slot A, New Game, then Yes to "Proceed with formatting?" -- the only CARDFormatAsync in the executable.
frames: 6000
pad: 1600:start,1640:a,1700:start
pad: 1730:start@30#5
pad: 1745:a+left@30#5
env: SOA_CARD=build/cards/blank.raw
env: SOA_CARD_VERBOSE=1
env: SOA_TRACE=1
env: SOA_TRACE_DUMP=1

shows: that the format offer is four presses from boot, not a save point deep
shows:   in the game. Choosing New Game runs the boot memory-card screen
shows:   (title scene 15, fn_8022AE94 over the object at 0x803167F0); its first
shows:   state calls card_scan, and a card whose mount answers -6 BROKEN gives
shows:   status 2, which is the one status that opens "The Memory Card in Slot A
shows:   is corrupt ... Proceed with formatting?" with Yes at x=224 and No at
shows:   x=344. LEFT moves the cursor to Yes, A confirms, and the next frame
shows:   card_format_step reaches 0x801A2758 -- the executable's only reference
shows:   to CARDFormatAsync, and its only caller of __CARDFormatRegionAsync.
shows: the write itself: five sector erases (command 0xF1) and five 8192-byte
shows:   blocks reprogrammed 128 bytes at a time (command 0xF2), so a completed
shows:   format should leave "card ... 40960 written" in the [exi] line and
shows:   build/cards/blank.raw on disk where nothing was before.
shows: with SOA_CARD pointed at a --damage'd image instead, the same script
shows:   shows the other write: card_scan CARDCheckExAsync repairs the one bad
shows:   system block, rewrites the status to 0 at 0x801A2A8C, and the screen
shows:   never draws. That is 8192 written and no prompt -- a different code
shows:   path, not a weaker version of this one.

evidence: THE ROUTE, from the jump table at 0x802F91AC, every arm read in the
evidence:   DOL and again in the generated C that actually runs: scene 11
evidence:   fn_80229124 tests the START bit of the pad EDGE word at
evidence:   [[0x80311A60]+8] (0x802292D8), and 0x802292EC is the ONLY store of
evidence:   12 to the scene word at 0x80311AE0; scene 12 fn_80228E00 stores
evidence:   fn_801D8EE8 id into byte 24 of the title object (0x80229074) and
evidence:   writes 13; scene 13 fn_80228C50 routes that byte -- 1 to scene 16
evidence:   (Continue), 119 to scene 2, anything else to 14 (0x80228DC0, the
evidence:   only store of 14); scene 14 fn_80228BE4 inits the screen object and
evidence:   writes 15 (0x80228C3C, the only store of 15); scene 15 fn_80228B74
evidence:   runs fn_8022AE94, the card screen.
evidence: WHY THE RUN OF 2026-09-18 NEVER GOT THERE, out of its own log. Scene
evidence:   10 reads START too: fn_802293E0 calls fn_802295D0, which at
evidence:   0x80229638 tests the same edge bit and ORs 0x80 into the five logo
evidence:   elements to SNAP the flyover, and scene 10 advances to 11 the moment
evidence:   that flyover reports finished (0x802294F4). So the first START
evidence:   offered to the logos is always spent skipping them.
evidence:   build/scenario-cardwrite.log lines 639-641 are exactly that:
evidence:   "[si] frame 1700 (retrace 3576): buttons 1000", then
evidence:   TitleSceneChange r4 0000000B, then "[si] frame 1710". Scene 11 was
evidence:   first dispatched one or two frames AFTER the press that caused it,
evidence:   with the edge already spent, and the script had no START left. Two
evidence:   STARTs on different frames are needed; one is never enough.
evidence: THE BUDGET. Scene 11 owned frames ~1702 to ~4505 (TitleSceneChange
evidence:   r4 0000000D sits between "[si] frame 4505" and the scene-2 line, log
evidence:   lines 858-860) and then bailed on its own attract timeout at
evidence:   0x80229230: 0x1686B = 92267 ms since the title stream started, which
evidence:   is HOST WALL CLOCK -- runtime/hle.c timebase() is timespec_get,
evidence:   scaled only by SOA_SPEED. It stored 119 into byte 24 at 0x802292C0
evidence:   and scene 13 sent 119 to scene 2, which is the ATTRACT DEMO, not the
evidence:   game: the log then runs 2,3,4,5,6,8, back to 0,1,9,10,11 and ends at
evidence:   frame 6000 still on the title. 2804 frames fitted in those 92.3 s,
evidence:   so this machine gives about 33 title frames per second -- a budget
evidence:   that shrinks on a slower machine and under SOA_SPEED, which is why
evidence:   every press here is packed into the first 250 frames of scene 11.
evidence: WHAT THE 81920 IN THE OTHER LOGS IS NOT. The note that used to stand
evidence:   here said a second mount proved those runs reached the card screen.
evidence:   In build/scenario-cardwrite.log both CardMountAsync lines carry
evidence:   lr 801A36B0 -- fn_801A3510, the title per-slot poll that fn_80228A20
evidence:   runs for scenes 9..13 -- and that run entered the title twice and
evidence:   never reached scene 15. Two mounts of 40960 is 81920 for that reason
evidence:   alone. The byte count proves nothing about the screen;
evidence:   CardScreenStatus (0x8022B828) firing is the only proof it ran, and
evidence:   it has never fired in this repository.
evidence: the card verdicts are the repo own model, not inference: "python
evidence:   tools/cardformat.py verify" on 512 KB of 0xFF prints "CARDDoMount
evidence:   would return -6 (BROKEN), first failure: CARDVerifyID device ID at
evidence:   +32". runtime/exi.c card_load() fills the buffer with 0xFF when
evidence:   SOA_CARD names a file that is not there, so "blank" costs nothing to
evidence:   make and sram_init() still presents its built-in CARD_FLASH_ID, so
evidence:   the mount is not diverted to -5 IOERROR. card_scan maps -6 and -13
evidence:   to status 2 at 0x801A2A2C, and on a blank image CARDVerifyID fails
evidence:   inside CARDCheckExAsync before any repair arm can run, so the status
evidence:   stays 2 and the prompt is what draws.

note: DELETE build/cards/blank.raw BEFORE EVERY RUN. A blank card is the
note:   absence of the file, and a successful format creates it (card_flush() in
note:   runtime/exi.c writes the image out as the pages are programmed). The
note:   second run would then find a valid card, card_scan would answer 0, the
note:   screen would never draw and nothing would be written. The file left
note:   behind is the proof, not the input: read it with "python
note:   tools/cardformat.py show build/cards/blank.raw".
note: AND DO NOT OVERRIDE SOA_CARD. The 2026-09-18 run was started with
note:   --env SOA_CARD=build/cards/damaged.raw ("[exi] memory card
note:   build/cards/damaged.raw (524288 bytes)" is line 6 of its log). A damaged
note:   image takes CARDCheckExAsync repair arm inside card_scan, which
note:   rewrites the status to 0 at 0x801A2A8C; the prompt then never appears
note:   and the write is an 8192-byte repair, not the format. That run is worth
note:   making once, deliberately, as its own line in the log -- it is not this
note:   one.
note: THE THREE PAD LINES, and what each press is for.
note:   1600:start,1640:a,1700:start reproduces the preamble of the run that was
note:   measured, so the scene timing in the evidence above still applies: 1600
note:   and 1640 land in the pre-title chain, and the START at 1700 is the one
note:   that snaps the logo flyover and puts scene 11 on screen at about frame
note:   1702.
note:   1730:start@30#5 is the press the old script never had: a fresh START
note:   edge every 30 frames from 1730, each held 5 frames. The first one that
note:   lands while scene 11 is the dispatched scene takes 0x802292EC and writes
note:   scene 12. If the flyover ran long and 1730 is spent snapping it instead,
note:   1760 or 1790 does the job -- the repeat is the whole point, because the
note:   snap always eats one press and the frame it is eaten on is not
note:   predictable to better than a few frames. Extra STARTs cost nothing:
note:   0x802292D8 and 0x80229638 are the only two tests of bit 0x1000 anywhere
note:   between 0x80228000 and 0x8022C000, so once scene 12 is up, START is dead
note:   input for the rest of the title and for the whole card screen.
note:   1745:a+left@30#5 is one press doing two different jobs, and it is
note:   pressed as A AND LEFT TOGETHER on purpose. See the next note.
note: WHY A AND LEFT MUST SHARE A FRAME, AND WHY THAT MAKES "No" UNREACHABLE.
note:   The prompt input is fn_8022B318 and it tests, in this order: LEFT
note:   (0x8022B41C, edge bit 0x1) -- and if the cursor is on No (obj[5] == 1)
note:   it toggles to Yes and RETURNS at 0x8022B45C without ever looking at A;
note:   then RIGHT; then A (0x8022B460), which reads Yes as confirm (0x8022B47C)
note:   and No as dismiss (0x8022B494, the arm that ends the boot). So a frame
note:   carrying BOTH bits is idempotent: on the cursor first frame it is a
note:   LEFT and the A is swallowed, and on the second it is an A on Yes,
note:   because the LEFT test falls through once the cursor is already Yes.
note:   Two of these presses, 30 frames apart, always format and can never
note:   dismiss -- whatever frame the fade gate at 0x8022B3E8 opens on. This is
note:   why the old "LEFT five frames before A" pattern was a gamble: if the
note:   gate opened inside that gap, the A was the first input the screen ever
note:   saw and it landed on No, with no second chance that boot.
note:   The same press is also the New Game confirm: fn_801D91F4 tests A first
note:   (0x801D92C8) and never reads LEFT at all (its cursor is UP/DOWN, bits
note:   0x8 and 0x4), so A+LEFT in the scene-12 menu is simply A.
note:   COROLLARY, and it matters: NO OTHER PAD LINE MAY PRESS LEFT. If a second
note:   line holds LEFT across the A frame, LEFT stops being a fresh edge there
note:   (the game reads [[0x80311A60]+8], which is cur & (cur ^ prev)) and the A
note:   reaches the cursor unprotected. The old 1800:left@30#5 and
note:   2920:left@150#5 lines are gone for that reason, not because they were
note:   redundant.
note: WHY NEW GAME IS ENTRY 0 AND CANNOT SILENTLY BECOME CONTINUE. fn_801D8D54
note:   memsets the 584-byte menu block, so the cursor byte starts at 0 = New
note:   Game, and scene 13 proves the mapping: id 1 goes to scene 16, which is
note:   fn_801D8B2C, the Continue/load flow. The one thing that moves the cursor
note:   by itself is fn_801D91F4 at 0x801D9254, which forces it to 1 on the
note:   first frame fn_801A3510 reports a save in slot A. It cannot fire here:
note:   fn_801A3510 returns 1 only with its flag bits 0x1 AND 0x2 both set, and
note:   0x2 is set only when fn_801A37D8 finds a save after a SUCCESSFUL mount.
note:   A blank card mounts -6, which takes 0x801A3634 instead -- flags
note:   0x10 | 0x1, a latched failure -- so the poll answers 0 all run. This
note:   script never presses UP or DOWN, so nothing else can move it.
note: the A repeat keeps working after the write. 0x8022B1E4 ("Formatting
note:   completed. Press A Button to continue.") takes an A and starts the game;
note:   0x8022B128 ("Formatting failed.") takes an A, re-scans and returns to
note:   the prompt WITHOUT resetting obj[20], so the cursor is still on Yes and
note:   the next A retries the format. Either way the run ends in the opening.
note: this scenario is headless on purpose -- no SOA_RENDER, so
note:   "scenario.py run cardwrite --check" reports the bad-vertex-reference
note:   invariant as skipped, the way capture.scn does. The card screen loads
note:   /title/ts900028.mld, which is the asset PROGRESS.md blames for the
note:   renderer crash that mounting the card first produced; nothing here needs
note:   a pixel, and the tracepoints say where the screen got to. Add
note:   SOA_RENDER=1 SOA_SNAP=50 with --env if you want to look at it.
note: SOA_TRACE_DUMP=1 is on for two sites that need it. CardScreenStatus is
note:   named @r31 so the dump is the 32-byte screen object at 0x803167F0 --
note:   word 0 the state, 2 what the screen returns to title scene 15, 3 the
note:   card status, 4 the slot, 5 the Yes/No cursor, 7 the fade sub-state that
note:   gates input. CardScanRepaired is named @r1 so word 4 of the dump is
note:   CARDCheckExAsync xferBytes out-param, which lives at r1+16 and is
note:   otherwise in r0, which trace.c does not print.
note: READING THE RESULT, with only the tracepoints already in config/trace.txt.
note:   TitleSceneChange (0x80228A60) r4 must show 0000000B, then 0000000C --
note:   the value this port has never printed -- then 0000000D, 0000000E,
note:   0000000F. No 0000000C means the STARTs still missed. 00000010 in place
note:   of 0000000E means the A took Continue, so the card was not blank.
note:   CardScreenStatus (0x8022B828) r3 = 00000002 is the prompt; 00000000 is a
note:   healthy or repaired card and the screen leaving without drawing.
note:   CardPromptCursor (0x8022B444) prints the edge word, so with this script
note:   it reads 00000101 (A+LEFT), not 00000001. CardPromptYes (0x8022B47C)
note:   fires once; CardPromptNo (0x8022B494) must never fire, and if it does
note:   the reading of fn_8022B318 above is wrong. Then CardFormatAsyncCall,
note:   CardFormatAsyncRet r3 0 or -1, five CardEraseSector, 320 CardWritePage,
note:   CardFormatResult r3 00000000, and "card ... 40960 written".
